I am applying for the Research Software Engineer position because I
enjoy building software for difficult problems where reliability,
careful engineering, and understanding the domain matter as much as
shipping features. The opportunity to contribute to research at NIST
strongly matches how I like to work.

\vspace{11bp}

The best example is
\href{https://www.paperlyft.com/}{\RSSemi Paperlyft}, an AI product I
built and deployed to convert scanned PDFs into accessible documents
while preserving their original content and layout. Building it from
an idea into a production system required much more than integrating
an LLM. I designed the document-processing workflow, validation and
repair stages, AWS infrastructure, model orchestration, and the
reliability checks needed for real documents. It taught me how to take
an ambiguous problem, test assumptions, make architectural tradeoffs,
and carry software all the way to production.

\vspace{11bp}

At Arizona State University's Learning Engineering Institute, I also
worked on Critical Code Reviewer, exploring how language models can
identify ethical issues in educational software. That work pushed me
to think carefully about model context, useful versus noisy findings,
evaluation, and how AI systems should fit into existing engineering
workflows.

\vspace{11bp}

My background is primarily in software and AI engineering rather than
traditional scientific research software, but I am comfortable
entering unfamiliar systems, reading existing work carefully, asking
the right questions, and learning the domain before deciding how to
build. I would be excited to bring that approach to the NIST PREP
environment and contribute to software researchers can depend on.

